---
/**
 * A post's hero image, generated at build time from the post itself.
 *
 * Replaces `PostCover.astro`, which drew a motif per topic varied by a hash of
 * the slug. The intent was right — no stock photography, nothing to keep in an
 * image pipeline — and the output was not: the "Tool design" motif rendered as
 * five coloured pills, which reads as a loading skeleton. That component's own
 * comment conceded one of its four motifs "read as an empty card".
 *
 * What replaces it is the same idea done from the post's own text: one template,
 * the title centred, the same background, palette and mark as the share card.
 * Built by `/og/hero/<slug>.png` from the front matter, so retitling a post
 * retitles its hero and there is no asset to regenerate by hand.
 *
 * ── Why a raster and not inline SVG ───────────────────────────────────────
 * Because the text has to be laid out. `PostCover` could be inline SVG because
 * it drew shapes; a centred, wrapped title needs a text engine, which is what
 * Satori is for at build time. The cost is a real image request instead of a
 * kilobyte of markup, which is why every one of these is lazy-loaded and
 * carries its own dimensions.
 */

interface Props {
  /** The post slug — `how-many-tools`. */
  slug: string;
  /** The post title. Only used to reason about the image, never rendered. */
  title: string;
  /**
   * Load immediately rather than lazily.
   *
   * True for the one image above the fold — a post's own hero, and the
   * featured card at the top of the index. The other 44 on the index page are
   * lazy, which is the difference between one image request and forty-five.
   *
   * This used to be folded into a single `density` prop alongside a variant
   * choice, and the two are unrelated: the index's featured card wants eager
   * loading *and* its title, and one prop could not say both.
   */
  eager?: boolean;
  class?: string;
}

const { slug, title, eager = false, class: klass = "" } = Astro.props;
---

{/*
  `alt=""` on purpose.

  The image contains the post's title and nothing else, and the title is already
  the `h1` directly above it. Reading it out twice is worse than not reading it
  at all — this is decorative in the precise sense the spec means.

  Dimensions are stated so the layout does not shift when it lands.

  `object-cover` because every call site sets its own aspect ratio — the index
  wants 2:1 cards, the post wants 5:2 from `sm` up. The image is 1200×630, so
  ~1.9:1, and cropping it to 2:1 takes three pixels off the top and bottom.
  The title is centred with room around it precisely so any of those crops are
  safe; a left-aligned card would lose words at the same ratios.
*/}
<img
  src={`/og/hero/${slug}.png`}
  alt=""
  width="1200"
  height="630"
  loading={eager ? "eager" : "lazy"}
  decoding="async"
  class:list={["w-full object-cover", klass]}
/>
